{
  "image_name": "0e5053d5e6b807abcd0c5a74cc4d49b2.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways engineered gut bacteria could treat disease by acting from the intestinal lumen and influencing distant organs or local inflammation. At the center is a human outline highlighting the gut, liver, and pancreas, with zoomed panels showing different therapeutic strategies.\n\nOne theme is immune modulation in the intestine. Engineered bacteria release an anti-inflammatory factor, IL-10, near inflamed epithelial cells. This appears to shift immune cell behavior toward regulatory responses, including Treg activity, while reducing proinflammatory signaling from T helper cells and macrophages. The overall result is a drop in cytokines such as IL-17, IL-6, IFNγ, and TNF.\n\nA second strategy targets metabolism: engineered microbes in the intestine produce GLP-1, which is shown acting on the pancreas, specifically islet cells, to increase insulin and decrease glucagon.\n\nA third pathway links intestinal bacterial products to the liver. Here, engineered bacteria produce IL-22, and the liver is associated with increased proliferative, anti-apoptotic, anti-oxidative, and antibacterial gene programs, along with reduced cellular senescence and lipogenic genes.\n\nThe final mechanism is local toxin defense: bacteria equipped with engineered surface receptors bind and neutralize toxins from harmful bacteria, helping prevent epithelial apoptosis.",
